# WHAT STANDS IN A VOID — a figure in a niche, a bed in a box.
#
# ⚠ NEITHER IS GIVEN A SIZE. The niche says how tall it is under its own arch and
# the figure is cut to it; the box says how wide it is and the bed takes that as its
# length. Photographed one at a time and close to, because in a wide shot a prop is
# a few pale pixels inside a dark hole and any of them would pass.

echo --- flat ground
at 0 0 0
key ArrowUp
wait rebuilt

echo --- one wall, with a niche in it
at -6 4 0
verb run
at 6 4 0
verb run
at -2 2.5 90
select 11
verb opening

echo --- and a figure standing in the niche, sized by the niche
select seat 1
verb seat

# ⚠ THE NICHE OPENS ON THE +z SIDE. A run's front face is the side its normal points
# at, which is NOT the side the author stood on to cut into it — and photographing
# the blind side gives a picture of an unbroken wall that looks like a prop that
# failed to draw. Twice now; hence this comment.
echo --- the figure, close to and on the side the niche opens
at -2 7.5 265
snap s9-statue

echo --- a box bed further along the same wall, and a bed in it
at 3 2.6 90
key J
select seat 0
verb seat

# ⚠ AND STAND ASIDE. The character is drawn where the author is and the camera sits
# behind it, so `at` directly in front of a void photographs the back of a person.
echo --- the bed, close to, from off the box's axis
at 6.5 1.0 118
snap s9-bed
